EN = [
"The Old City Underground",
"Cut white stone shows through the blue-black growth covering the wall. A single letter on it. The first letter of the rubbing.",
"…This is the cave. Where I said 'let's go down,' twenty years ago. It looks narrower than it did then.",
"Look at the wall. Black lines run across it. See where they spread from.",
"There's a lump of metal on the floor. …Wait. I look first.",
"The inner wall. Where the humming is. The stone's a different color.",
"We've seen all there is to see. Let's go up. I've got something to ask Bram.",
"Iron Axe",
"Bram the Smith: Striking that thing head-on is no use. It's stone. Your blade will chip. The only thing that harms it is its own lightning.",
"Bram the Smith: They say crystals rise on both sides before it looses its long bolt. Break those crystals with an axe. A sword won't do it. It's the grain. Break one, and the lightning pooled in the crystal passes into the body of whoever broke it.",
"Bram the Smith: A body carrying lightning isn't harmed when its bolt strikes. Stand in that state before the mirror stone on the wall. When it shoots at you, the bolt passes through you, hits the mirror stone, and goes back to it.",
"▷ You're telling me to get struck by lightning on purpose?",
"Bram the Smith: That's right. A smith does it every day. To put your hand in the fire, you wet the hand first. Just don't get the order wrong.",
"Bram the Smith: And the four standing stones. They rise around it when it gets up, I hear. At the end it draws the strength of all four at once and bursts it. Knock the standing stones down before that. Every one you fell will make it hurt that much less.",
"Bram the Smith: Don't so much as look up overhead. The air above it is all lightning. Jumping is fine, but fly and you die.",
"Expedition Captain Roran: The archivist asked me. Why the captain always counts people before heading out. She thought it was a habit.",
"Expedition Captain Roran: It's not a habit. It's a punishment. I didn't count here. If I had, I'd have known two had fallen behind. Thomas was always late because he was seeing to Lia's bootlaces.",
"Expedition Captain Roran: That's why I check the new recruits' bootlaces three times. The archivist complained to you about it. I know. That child wrote it down as my nagging.",
"▷ What will you say to the Elder?",
"Expedition Captain Roran: Nothing. Not yet. Kael spent twenty years keeping me alive. I can't throw that away on one day's anger. Instead I'll touch the thing that man said not to touch. That is my answer.",
"Expedition Captain Roran: The thing down there isn't a guardian, they say. Then there's no fear of a seal bursting. We go without a Gate. Same as twenty years ago. This time with our eyes open.",
"Chesed, the Stone That Was Given No Name",
"Wardens of the Deep City",
"Chapter Two · Beneath the First Cave",
"Twenty years ago, where the expedition first lost someone",
"Chronicle of Vestalia · The Discarded Stone",
"The eighth stone. In place of a name, one word: Chesed (mercy)\nSpace for a name of eight letters, left empty\nBefore it, two helmets. Thomas, Lia. Standing back to back.",
"Chronicle of Vestalia · The Expedition of Twenty Years Ago",
"Thomas · Lia · Bren · Marco · Ines\nDanilo · Jonah · Fei · Hayun · Otto\nKael, second in command. The one who vanished to save a friend\nAnd Roran, captain. The one who came back and counted heads",
"Elder Erwin: You gathered all eleven. …And Roran walked it himself, I hear. On that leg. A remarkable man. Truly.",
"Elder Erwin: Did you burn the roster? It is the custom to burn it when an expedition ends.",
"▷ Isabel wrote it into the Chronicle.",
"Elder Erwin: …Did she. (He adjusts his glove.) That child writes everything down. I taught her to. It was well done. Probably.",
"Elder Erwin: What did Kael say at the end? …No, never mind. It is not good manners to pry into a dead man's words.",
"Elder Erwin: …He went down beneath the lode. Roran did. (He sets down his teacup. It makes a sound.) What was carved on that stone?",
"▷ One word. Chesed.",
"Elder Erwin: Mercy. …Yes. So that sculptor did his work to the end. And the place for the name. Was it empty? Yes. It would be.",
"Head count. Twelve. …For the first time in twenty years, the number is right.",
"Oscar the Fisher: The captain was a good person. Knew every sailor's name by heart. Three hundred of them. The first day Elric went aboard, the captain called him 'Elric, Oscar's son,' and that boy talked of nothing else all evening.",
"Oscar the Fisher: That ship had one law. 'No one dies on my ship.' Sailors heard that one line and signed on. That's why I let him go, too.",
"Oscar the Fisher: The town knows that night better than I do. I can't remember that night at all. I only know I was standing on the dock. If you want to know, ask the people. Everyone has a piece of it.",
"Oscar the Fisher: …Don't say 'find' so lightly. Once you find, it's over. Even so, let's go.",
"Oscar the Fisher, a lantern lit for fifteen years",
"Raw Salmon",
"The Lantern on the Ice",
"A brass lantern hangs on a stake driven into the ice. Its glass is wiped clean, without a trace of soot. Footprints are worn into the ice at the foot of the stake. The depth of one person standing in the same place thousands of times.",
"Oscar the Fisher: Elric felt the cold. Wound two scarves round his neck. And yet he said he'd go to sea. On a ship for the northern waters.",
"Oscar the Fisher: I tried to stop him. Three days I tried. On the fourth he said to me, 'Old man, they say no one dies on that ship.' With that one line, I lost.",
"Oscar the Fisher: The day he left I waved from the dock. He looked back once, and that was all. Sailors are like that. I waved until the ship was out of sight.",
"Oscar the Fisher: That was the last of it. We didn't quarrel, and I didn't give him any fine words. I just waved. That's why I light the lantern. In place of my hand.",
"Bram the Smith: That night? My forge went out. Once in my whole life. I worked the bellows and the fire wouldn't take. The air had frozen. Something came pushing in from the sea all at once. Not the cold. The thing that comes before the cold.",
"Bram the Smith: Oscar, you mean. I replace the brass on that man's lantern every year. I don't take payment. When I try, he leaves fish behind, so I just count that.",
"Bram the Smith: No one in this town has a word to say about that man going out on the ice every night. Everyone lost someone that night. They envy a person who has somewhere to go.",
"Lorenzo the Restaurant Owner: That evening? I had soup on the boil. For three hundred! It was the day the captain was due back. On the days that ship came in, the whole town ate at my place.",
"Lorenzo the Restaurant Owner: The soup froze as it was. Pot and all. Over the fire. I still have that pot. I couldn't bring myself to throw it out.",
"Lorenzo the Restaurant Owner: Mr. Oscar hasn't touched hot soup since. I've offered for fifteen years. He only eats it cold. I asked him why once, and he said, 'Hot food is for eating with the one you were waiting for.'",
"Morris the Guide: I was ten then. I was playing on the dock when I saw it. The horizon standing up white. It wasn't a wave. It was ice, walking toward us.",
"Morris the Guide: The fishermen launched their boats. To go and save the captain's ship, they said. I clapped. Because it was grand. …I didn't know those boats wouldn't come back.",
"Morris the Guide: That's why I'm out on the dock every time a Survey Corps ship comes in. There ought to be at least one person counting the ships that come in.",
"Leather",
"Outpost of the Ice Maze",
"Six bowls are set on the table. The soup has frozen, bowl and all. One spoon hangs in the air above a bowl. Along with the hand that held it.",
"Six people are frozen where they sit. Not with startled faces. One of them is smiling. Someone had just been telling a joke.",
"There is soot on the apron of the youngest. A smithy apron. Three hinge nails in the pocket.",
"Oscar the Fisher: …So it was in an instant. If it was like this for Elric too. If it didn't hurt. Is it all right to call that a mercy?",
"A duty roster hangs beside the door. The last line: \"Storm on the northern horizon. The captain's ship went that way. Nothing to report.\"",
"Below it, one line written hastily in another hand: \"Leave the door open. People are walking in across the ice. Many lights.\" He wrote this line, and picked up his spoon.",
"A recipe is written on the back of the roster. Spinefish soup. \"To be fed to those who come in off the ice. Warms from the inside first. Cook six bowls at a time.\"",
"Igloo on the Ice",
"Six bowls are set on the table. The soup has frozen, bowl and all. Six people are frozen where they sit. One of them is smiling.",
"The outpost. The ice has climbed right up to the roof. The door is… open.",
"Out of the way, I'm throwing!",
"I meant to use the harpoon only on fish!",
"Fall here and you won't get up! Hold on!",
"There's a table inside. Still laid. Let's go closer.",
"Something's hanging by the door. A duty roster. Read the last line.",
"Let's tell it as we saw it. That the door was open, too. Go on ahead.",
"Hideout of the Ice Maze",
"Writing on the Wall · Thirty",
"Letters carved into the ice wall with the point of a knife. The strokes are clear. Written while the hand was still sound.",
"\"Ship caught in the ice. Captain told us to get off and walk. Thirty got off. Captain stayed with the ship. Said, 'I will bring the rest myself.'\"",
"\"Can see the town's lights on the horizon. Can see boats putting out from the dock too. Coming to fetch us. We quicken our pace.\"",
"Writing on the Wall · Eighteen",
"The strokes of the second writing waver. It is not the same hand. The first could no longer write, and the next took over.",
"\"Saw the boats from town get caught in the ice. On their way to fetch us. If we hadn't got off and waved our lights, they would not have come out.\"",
"\"That sea route was sold by the scholar in the grey cloak. The captain paid the price of a ship. The scholar called it 'the eighth eye, the power to bring back what is forgotten.' The captain meant to win back the Morning Star.\"",
"\"In the middle of the storm someone dropped the anchor. Out of fear. The anchor struck something below. There was the sound of a bell. From under the sea. And then it froze.\"",
"Writing on the Wall · Seven",
"The third writing is shallow. Scratched with a fingernail, with no strength left to hold a knife.",
"\"Don't blame the one who dropped the anchor. I'd have dropped it too.\"",
"\"Don't blame the captain. We chose to follow.\"",
"\"Sorry to the boats from town. I'm the one who waved the light. — the seven left of the thirty who got off\"",
"Tormented Souls Blocking the Way",
"Strays of the Ice Maze",
"Chronicle of Vestalia · Names Beneath the Ice",
"Hassan, good at mending nets\nMare, couldn't sing",
"Anglerfish Lantern",
"Tormented Souls Holding Oscar",
"Drowned in the Cracks of the Ice",
"Elder Guardian Under the Ice",
"I'll be at the door. With the lantern lit. This time I'm lighting it not for that boy, but for you to see.",
"Mother of the Maze\nA fish of the deep, trapped in the ice and starving. It gave back every voice it had swallowed.\nElric, Oscar's son",
"The Stone Marker on the Ice",
"A stone the size of a palm sits beside the lantern stake. Half sunk into the ice. Fifteen winters have pushed it in.",
"You hang the name tag on the stone. The scrap of metal taps the stone in the wind. A small sound. And yet at that sound Oscar lifts his head.",
"Oscar the Fisher: …It sounds like that boy knocking at the door. On the nights he came in late. He knocked three times. Carefully.",
"Sharks Under the Ice",
"Great Eels",
"The Frozen Prows",
"Prows jut up through the ice. Fishing boats. Every one faces north. The sails are furled. They were rowed here. It was a night when the wind had died.",
"Every prow has a name carved on it. \"Seagull.\" \"Third Daughter.\" \"Late Riser.\" \"The Day the Debt Was Paid.\" The kind of names fishermen give their boats. Count them and there are nineteen.",
"At the very back there is one more. The twentieth. It has no name. The place for carving one has only been planed smooth. And yet its oars are shipped. Someone rowed it. All the way here.",
"A scarf is frozen to the bottom of the nameless boat. A scarf long enough to wind round twice. A spare.",
"Drowned of the Boat Graveyard",
"Oscar the Fisher: Nineteen boats went out. I stood on the dock. Afraid. Because I had seen the ice walking toward us.",
"Oscar the Fisher: Then it came to me. That the boy had gone with only one scarf. The boy who always wound two. I grabbed the spare and ran and pushed out a boat. A new one with no name carved on it yet.",
"Oscar the Fisher: I got as far as that graveyard. I watched the nineteen boats ahead get caught, one after another. Without a sound. Men at the oars going stiff with the oars still raised.",
"Oscar the Fisher: I let go of the oars. I got out of the boat. And I walked toward town. I left the scarf in the boat.",
"▷ That wasn't running away.",
"Oscar the Fisher: It was running away. Only, because I ran, one person was left to light a lantern. What to call that, I don't know myself. Ask the young archivist. Whether there's a word for writing that kind of thing down.",
"Archivist Isabel: \"Merchant ship Morning Star, lost in the northern sea. Captain Cornelia, twenty-two, first voyage. Crew of thirty-two.\"",
"Archivist Isabel: \"Ten days later the captain was found alone, adrift on a plank. Statement: 'The thing that came up from below took the whole ship in its arms and went down. It did not break it. It carried it off in its arms.'\"",
"Archivist Isabel: \"The captain called out the thirty-two names without a single mistake. The archivist took them down.\" …This archivist is my mother. The handwriting.",
"▷ So Elena met Captain Cornelia.",
"Archivist Isabel: There's one more line in the margin, in my mother's hand. \"This person lives because she cannot forget. Someday, I think, someone will use that.\" …My mother knew. More than fifteen years ago.",
"Archivist Isabel: There are drawings on the back pages. The captain drew them. They say that for ten days after she came back alive, it was all she drew. The paper is so decayed I can't open it. I'll steam it open. It will take a few days.",
"Pirate Ship Trapped in the Ice",
"The Deck of the Pirate Ship",
"The middle of the deck has been torn out in a circle. Not broken. The edges are crushed inward. Something wound round it, squeezed, and lifted.",
"Sucker marks are stamped in a row along the rail. Each one the size of a shield. The wood is dented in that shape.",
"Words carved hastily with a dagger into the door of the captain's cabin: \"Came up from under the ice. Didn't break the ship. Hugged it. As if weighing it. Then pulled out the mast and took it. Left us. No interest in people.\"",
"Below it, another hand: \"Each time it comes back the ship gets a little lighter. Anchor. Rudder. Cannon. It takes them one by one. We have to stay here until it has taken the whole ship.\"",
"Starving Pirates",
"Pillagers on the Ice",
"That ship. Do you see it? The middle is heaved up. A ship caught in ice doesn't end up like that.",
"The deck's been torn out. Look at the edges. Not broken. Crushed.",
"Pirates are sailors too. A starving sailor is a pitiful thing. Even so, the blades have to be put away.",
"I can tell what did this. Let's go back and ask the young archivist to show us the drawings.",
"The yellowed paper is crowded with charcoal drawings. The same thing drawn dozens of times. A round heap on the sea floor. Up close, it is all ships. Masts, keels, prows. Hundreds of them woven together.",
"In the middle of the heap something huge lies curled. Its arms are wrapped round the heap. Not the posture of fighting. The posture of brooding over something. Its eyes are closed.",
"Only one ship, at the very heart of the heap, is drawn sharply. A star on its prow. Thirty-two small figures on its deck. Each drawn differently. Height, hat, even the way they stand.",
"Shaking letters in the corner of the drawing: \"It loves ships. That is why it takes them. I cannot hate it. That is the hardest part.\"",
"Archivist Isabel: …They say a ship that's held like that doesn't rot. Uncle Oscar told me. It's an old saying among the fishermen. Then the Morning Star is still just as it was. Just as it was thirty years ago.",
"The Channel the Pirates Broke",
"Black water lies open between the ice. The way the pirate ship broke its way in. The water is deep. Far down in the depths, something slowly turns over in its sleep.",
]
